```java
public interface Command {
    void execute();
    void undo();
}

public class CommandHistory {
    private final Deque<Command> history = new ArrayDeque<>();

    public void execute(Command cmd) {
        cmd.execute();
        history.push(cmd);
    }

    public void undo() {
        if (!history.isEmpty()) history.pop().undo();
    }
}
```

Every command carries its own inverse — a `MoveShapeCommand` knows both the new position and the old one, so `undo()` is just moving back, never a lookup into some separate historical snapshot.

```java
public class MacroCommand implements Command {
    private final List<Command> commands;

    public void execute() { commands.forEach(Command::execute); }
    public void undo() {
        for (int i = commands.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) commands.get(i).undo();
    }
}
```

Undo runs in **reverse order** of execute — if command B depended on A having already run, undoing must reverse B before A. Wrapping several commands as one `MacroCommand` also means the history stack treats "drag and resize" as a single undoable step, not two.
